{"authored" [{"question" "e-21717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny = x\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}-1\\right)^2 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nIf $y'(x) = t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t), y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t) = x(t) f(t) + g(t)\n$$\n\nFind $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} - \\dfrac{2(t-1)}{t-(t-1)^2}x = \\dfrac{2}{t-(t-1)^2},\\qquad t-(t-1)^2\\ne0.\n$$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-21717/review.json" "file_sha256" "2ea5fa8d621efd1f849bff1f22b2b6b41ed9ba0a739a2c5d41a9707e37c4e683" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-21717/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "d4d1a96456f1f43dbd4007230bf044c9b82a30fa3c6325bade208cdc9a14ddf8" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-21717/verification.json" "basis" 1926 "rationale" "Retain the original equation and coefficient-identification answer t²+1. Replace the false supplied elementary x(t) with x(t) and derive the correct transformed linear equation from the original ODE; give a verified integrating-factor quadrature on valid parameter intervals. Raw canonical DOM and screenshot remain unchanged."} {"question" "e-21717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Substitute $t=y'(x)$ into the original differential equation:\n\n$$y=x(t-1)^2+2t.$$\n\nTherefore $f(t)=(t-1)^2$ and $g(t)=2t$, so\n\n$$f(t)+g(t)=(t-1)^2+2t=t^2+1.$$\n\nFor the nonconstant parameter branch, differentiate with respect to $t$ and use $dy/dt=t\\,dx/dt$:\n\n$$t x'=(t-1)^2x'+2(t-1)x+2.$$\n\nWriting $D(t)=t-(t-1)^2$ yields the corrected linear equation\n\n$$x'-\\frac{2(t-1)}{D(t)}x=\\frac{2}{D(t)},\\qquad D(t)\\ne0.$$\n\nOn any interval avoiding the roots of $D$, choose a base point $t_0$ and define\n\n$$I(t)=\\exp\\!\\left(\\int_{t_0}^{t}\\frac{-2(s-1)}{D(s)}\\,ds\\right).$$\n\nThen\n\n$$x(t)=\\frac{C+\\int_{t_0}^{t}2I(s)/D(s)\\,ds}{I(t)},\\qquad y(t)=x(t)(t-1)^2+2t.$$\n\nIndeed $(Ix)'=2I/D$, so these formulas satisfy the corrected linear equation and $dy/dt=t\\,dx/dt$. They define a local graph where $x'(t)\\ne0$.\n\nThe excluded constant slopes $r=(3\\pm\\sqrt5)/2$ satisfy $r=(r-1)^2$ and give the separate straight-line solutions $y=rx+2r$. The original elementary expression for $x(t)$ and its associated hint are incorrect for the original equation." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-21717/review.json" "file_sha256" "2ea5fa8d621efd1f849bff1f22b2b6b41ed9ba0a739a2c5d41a9707e37c4e683" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-21717/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "d4d1a96456f1f43dbd4007230bf044c9b82a30fa3c6325bade208cdc9a14ddf8" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-21717/verification.json" "basis" 1926 "rationale" "Retain the original equation and coefficient-identification answer t²+1. Replace the false supplied elementary x(t) with x(t) and derive the correct transformed linear equation from the original ODE; give a verified integrating-factor quadrature on valid parameter intervals. Raw canonical DOM and screenshot remain unchanged."} {"question" "e-21717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny = x\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}-1\\right)^2 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nIf $y'(x) = t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t), y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\left( \\dfrac{2\\ln|t| - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2} \\right) f(t) + g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/example-21717.json" "file_sha256" "af22e5b52513c6e08f91dc33de01eeafbeb4a4accf3d64eae61eed3a1bcc62ef" "basis" 1924 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution $y'(x) = t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =(t-1)^{2}\\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =\\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{2\\ln|t| - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny = x(t-1)^2 + 2t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}})(t-1)^{2}+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)=(t-1)^2,\\ g(t)=2t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t) = t^2 +1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/example-21717.json" "file_sha256" "af22e5b52513c6e08f91dc33de01eeafbeb4a4accf3d64eae61eed3a1bcc62ef" "basis" 1924 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14055149/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [] "usage" {"e-21717" []} "basis" 1926 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
